  now(), now(), 5, false, 'Class of 2007-2009: Europe''s best youngsters, and Ismaël Camara, the jewel of Lille', 'Yamal, Estêvão, Cubarsí, Dowman… Players born between 2007 and 2009 are already taking over. In the Youth League, one name is shining brighter and brighter: Ismaël Camara, a Lille winger born in 2008 who came through Brio Youth Academy. Passe D presents the world top 15 and the talents to watch.', '<p>They are 17, 18 or 19, and they are no longer waiting their turn. Players born between 2007 and 2009 form an exceptional generation: some are already winning trophies with the pros, others are lighting up the Youth League, the under-19 Champions League. Among them, a former <strong>Brio Youth Academy</strong> player is making a name for himself in Europe: <strong>Ismaël Camara</strong>.</p>
<h2>Ismaël Camara, the jewel of Lille</h2>
<p>Remember the name. <strong>Ismaël Camara</strong>, born on 10 July 2008 in Bondy, is one of the revelations of this Youth League. A winger or attacking midfielder, able to play on the left, on the right or through the middle, he has everything of the modern player: pace, directness, vision and the composure you only see in the very best talents.</p>
<p>His numbers speak for him. In four Youth League games with Lille, he has played every minute (360 out of 360), scored <strong>2 goals</strong> and provided <strong>1 assist</strong>.</p>
<p>His masterpiece came in London on 13 October, away to Arsenal (a 3-2 win). First a pure strike from 20 yards, tipped out of the top corner by the goalkeeper. Then, from the resulting corner, a goal scored directly, an inswinging delivery nobody touched. Before the break, he set off on a surging run and laid on the third goal for <strong>Saad Boussadia</strong>. A goal, an assist and the man of the match award. No less.</p>
<p>Eight days later, he did it again, opening the scoring against Galatasaray (3-1). Even in the defeat at Bodø/Glimt (2-1), he was one of Lille''s most dangerous players until the end.</p>
<p>His path is that of a hard worker. He started at Bobigny, then FC 93, came through <strong>Brio Youth Academy</strong>, joined Paris FC in 2023, and was then signed by Lille in January 2026. Today he also plays for Lille''s reserve team and, according to Africafoot, he trains regularly with the professional squad and is knocking on the first-team door.</p>
<p>At Passe D, we say it without hesitation: <strong>Ismaël Camara is one of the five best European players born in 2008</strong>. Elegant, decisive, intelligent: a pure talent, a diamond Lille are polishing. Everyone at Brio Youth Academy can be proud. 👏</p>
<h2>The world top 15 of players born since 2007</h2>
<p>To place this generation, here is the reference ranking: GOAL''s NXGN 2026, which every year ranks the world''s best players born on or after 1 January 2007. Clubs are those in the ranking, published in March 2026.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Lamine Yamal</strong> (Spain, FC Barcelona). Winner for the third time. In a class of his own.</li>
<li><strong>Estêvão</strong> (Brazil, Chelsea).</li>
<li><strong>Pau Cubarsí</strong> (Spain, FC Barcelona). The best defender of his generation.</li>
<li><strong>Franco Mastantuono</strong> (Argentina, Real Madrid).</li>
<li><strong>Lennart Karl</strong> (Germany, Bayern Munich).</li>
<li><strong>Max Dowman</strong> (England, Arsenal). Born on 31 December 2009, he is the youngest on the list.</li>
<li><strong>Luka Vušković</strong> (Croatia, Hamburg, on loan from Tottenham).</li>
<li><strong>Ayyoub Bouaddi</strong> (Morocco, Lille at the time of the ranking). Another talent who came through Lille.</li>
<li><strong>Geovany Quenda</strong> (Portugal, Sporting CP).</li>
<li><strong>Ethan Nwaneri</strong> (England, Marseille, on loan from Arsenal).</li>
<li><strong>Rodrigo Mora</strong> (Portugal, FC Porto).</li>
<li><strong>Honest Ahanor</strong> (Italy, Atalanta).</li>
<li><strong>Ibrahim Mbaye</strong> (Senegal, Paris SG). Born in 2008, like Ismaël Camara.</li>
<li><strong>Konstantinos Karetsas</strong> (Greece, Genk).</li>
<li><strong>Rio Ngumoha</strong> (England, Liverpool). Also born in 2008.</li>
</ol>
<p>Just behind come Mexico''s <strong>Gilberto Mora</strong> (16th, born in 2008), Spain''s <strong>Dro Fernández</strong> (18th, Paris SG) and France''s <strong>Quentin Ndjantou</strong> (35th, Paris SG).</p>
<h2>Youth League: the players shining this season</h2>
<p>The Youth League is the shop window for these youngsters. After four rounds, three teams have a perfect record: Real Madrid, Feyenoord and Slavia Prague, with 12 points out of 12. Here are the players making headlines:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Will Wright</strong> (Liverpool) and <strong>Enis Redzepi</strong> (Stuttgart), top scorers with 6 goals each.</li>
<li><strong>Saad Boussadia</strong> (Lille), 4 goals, Ismaël Camara''s partner in attack.</li>
<li><strong>Adam Ayari</strong> (Paris SG) and <strong>Lorenzo Paratici</strong> (AS Roma), 4 goals each.</li>
<li><strong>Daniel Yáñez</strong> (Real Madrid), who has reached 10 career goals in the competition.</li>
<li><strong>Iván Corralejo</strong> (Betis), top provider with 4 assists.</li>
<li><strong>JJ Gabriel</strong> (Manchester United), 50th in the NXGN and one of the tournament''s highest-rated players.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What next?</h2>
<p>With 7 points from 4 games, Lille are well placed to qualify. Two gala fixtures remain: at home to <strong>Bayern Munich</strong> on 25 November, then <strong>Stuttgart</strong> on 9 December. Two perfect games for Ismaël Camara to show all of Europe once again what he can do.</p>
<p>Passe D will be watching very closely. And something tells us we have not finished writing his name. ⭐</p>
